  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-20T07:30:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "b7b9ceaa-ed7c-4bc3-a00b-d8ae80379751",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 200730\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1230 AM PDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming trough brings widespread rain and mountain snow \n  chances, gusty winds, and cooling temperatures this week. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and seasonable \n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nOur next system begins to encroach on the region today in the form \nof a closed low spinning off the CA coast. Over the remainder of the \nwork week, it will slowly move through the forecast area, eventually \ntransitioning to an open wave as it phases with a PacNW shortwave. \nNorthward moisture advection and upper-level divergence in the left \nexit region of a jet streak will spur isolated to scattered showers \nand storm today. Best chances (25-50%) are generally northwest of I-\n15 and in Mohave County. The slow-moving nature of the system \nresults in a similar setup on Wednesday. Precipitation chances are \nhigher across the board (+25%) on Wednesday due to additional \nmoisture and colder air aloft, but the same places will be favored. \nFinally, the main vort max associated with the low swings through on \nThursday, bringing the best potential for widespread precipitation \n(60-90% in most locations). However, the phasing with the PacNW \nshortwave sends a secondary vort max down the backside and keeps the \ntrough overhead through the end of the work week. As a result, \nisolated to scattered shower chances linger on Friday. \n\nOverall, best odds of precipitation impacts are in the southern \nGreat Basin, Inyo County, and northern Mohave County where chances \nof 1\"+ range from 25-50%. Thunderstorm development will aid this \npotential, but training convection is going to be required given the \nflow aloft. The biggest \"bust\" potential is in the southern Mojave \nDesert (think of the area bounded between Twentynine Palms, Barstow, \nLas Vegas, Kingman, and Lake Havasu), where models have this zone \nkind of caught in a dry slot between the two main precipitation \nregimes. This area has a 40-70% chance of 0.10\" or less. There is \na minor wintry component of this system as snow levels bottom out \naround 8kft in the Sierra. However, as they do, precipitation will\nlargely be done, so any impacts are expected to be confined to \nelevations above 9kft where forecast snow totals range from 3-10\" \n(the higher amounts near the crest). Lastly, gusty south-southwest\nwinds (20-40 mph) will accompany this system as well, but any \nimpacts should be confined to the local lakes.\n\nBy Saturday, ensemble guidance has our area firmly on the backside \nof the trough. We will see a dry northerly flow that quickly scours \nout moisture and precipitation chances, and produces some gusty \nnorth winds. On Sunday and Monday, the flow weakens and becomes more \nzonal/southwesterly, keeping us dry with light winds and slightly \nbelow-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package... \nFavorable conditions are expected through Tuesday, with winds \nfollowing typical diurnal directional trends and speeds generally \nremaining less than 8 knots. Northeast winds Tuesday morning will \nturn more southeasterly by 21Z. There is a 15 percent chance of \nseeing an isolated gust to 12 knots once winds turn southeast, but \nany gusts should be short-lived. Isolated convection is possible \nduring the afternoon and early evening over the higher terrain \nsurrounding the valley, but no precipitation is expected near the \nfield. VFR conditions will prevail through Tuesday night, with SCT \nclouds and bases generally remaining AOA 10kft AGL. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical diurnal directional trends are expected at area TAF sites \novernight into Tuesday morning. Most sites will see light winds \ncontinue into Tuesday afternoon. One exception will be across the \nnorthern Owens Valley, including KBIH, where southerly winds gusting \n30 to 35 knots are expected during the afternoon and early evening. \nGusty easterly winds are also expected across the western Mojave \nDesert, including KDAG. Isolated areas of convection will be seen \nover higher terrain during the afternoon and early evening, but no \ndirect impacts are expected at area terminals.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
